Builds two tables (types and constructs) from Pass A output for the programmatic corpus path, replacing the expensive `O(T^s)` enumeration of Pass B with a replay strategy that costs only as much as strokes pressed. The type table holds appendable `@type` definitions; the construct table holds count-resolved entries with slot positions and fill order, ready for runtime template replay via the walk module.

`build_types(entries) -> Result<Vec<InfType>, ExpandError>` filters `@type` entries and renders their templates (which may contain only literals and `%t` markers) into `InfType` records holding stroke, arity, and text. `build_constructs(entries) -> Result<Vec<Construct>, ExpandError>` runs Pass A count expansion on all non-`@type` entries and builds a `Construct` record from each, extracting template slot positions and computing fill order — for fused constructs (those with `@fuse` and at least one type slot), the fuse-target slot leads the fill order (D12 rule), otherwise template order applies. `build_tables` calls both and returns the pair.

`Construct.base` is the stroke prefix (minus the shape segment for fused constructs); `shape` is the final segment if `@fuse` applies, and the fuse-target type's stroke merges into it at runtime. `check_fuse_ambiguity(types, constructs) -> Result<(), ExpandError>` enforces criterion 5: no two `(shape, type)` pairs may merge to the same stroke under the same base, an invariant that enumeration used to catch as output collisions but must now be checked explicitly or silent wrong lookups occur. The `walk` module replays type-append using these tables; changes to slot semantics or fill order must stay in sync with callers of `template_ops` and `render_walk`.